  pages={25338-25353},
  abstract={This paper presents a DTC-based snapshot all-digital phase-locked loop (ADPLL) that achieves robust fractional spur suppression and low phase noise with compact circuit implementation. The proposed architecture employs a constant-slope capacitor digital-to-analog converter (CDAC)-based digital-to-time converter (DTC) to narrow the effective range of the time-to-digital converter (TDC), thereby enabling high-resolution phase detection without requiring a wide dynamic range. A feedforward spur cancellation filter suppresses residual fractional errors caused by DTC gain error and nonlinearity. In addition, an asynchronous duty-modulation unit (DMU) dithers the LSB of the digitally controlled oscillator (DCO) to reduce DCO quantization noise and distribute it across finer time segments, consequently lowering the integrated RMS jitter. Fabricated in a 65-nm CMOS process, the prototype ADPLL consumes 6.92 mW in steady-state operation, achieving 0.73 ps integrated RMS jitter and–71.6 dBc worst-case fractional spur. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of combining snapshot-based phase detection, feedforward spur cancellation, and asynchronous pulse-width dithering of DCO.},
